  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Give example proving not function</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text>&lt;p&gt;We define the relation, \(f\), to be the subset of \( \mathbb{Z} \times \mathbb{Z} \) such that&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-left: 30px"&gt;\( f=\{ ({@aa@}mn, {@bb*m+cc*n@}) : m,n \in \mathbb{Z} \} \)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;br&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Find two pairs of integers \((m_1, n_1)\) and \((m_2, n_2)\) that take the same value in the domain of the relation, but different values in the range.
&lt;br&gt;This then shows that \(f\) is &lt;b&gt;not&lt;/b&gt; a function.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="margin-left: 30px"&gt;\( m_{1} = \) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] and \( n_{1} = \) [[input:ans1s]] [[validation:ans1s]]&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p style="margin-left: 30px"&gt;\( m_{2} = \) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]  and \( n_{2} = \) [[input:ans2s]] [[validation:ans2s]]&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;[[feedback:prt1]]&lt;/p&gt;</text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text>&lt;h3&gt;Worked solution&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We have the relation,&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-left: 30px"&gt;\( f=\{ ({@aa@}mn, {@bb*m+cc*n@}) : m,n \in \mathbb{Z} \} \)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We require two pairs of integer coordinates \((m,n)\) where the values in the domain of the relation, \({@aa*m*n@}\), are the same. 
&lt;br&gt;For this we can choose any number with a non-unique factorization, so any non-prime integer, then take \(m\) and \(n\) to be the different pairs of factors.
&lt;br&gt;For example, choosing {@t_ans1*t_ans1s@} we have distinct factorizations, 

&lt;p style="margin-left: 30px"&gt;\({@t_ans1*t_ans1s@} = {@t_ans1@} \times {@t_ans1s@}\)
&lt;br&gt;\({@t_ans1*t_ans1s@} = {@t_ans2@} \times {@t_ans2s@}\)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;br&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We also require that the value of the pairs of integers are different in the range of the relation, \({@bb*m + cc*n@}\),
&lt;br&gt;We can verify that this is the case in the above example.

&lt;p style="margin-left: 30px"&gt;For \((m,n) = ({@t_ans1@}, {@t_ans1s@})\), we have \({@bb*m+cc*n = bb*t_ans1+cc*t_ans1s@}\)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-left: 30px"&gt;For \((m,n) = ({@t_ans2@}, {@t_ans2s@})\), we have \({@bb*m+cc*n = bb*t_ans2+cc*t_ans2s@}\)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Therefore we may take&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-left: 30px"&gt;\((m_1,n_1) = ({@t_ans1@}, {@t_ans1s@})\)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-left: 30px"&gt;\((m_2,n_2) = ({@t_ans2@}, {@t_ans2s@})\)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;br&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In particular, \(f\) cannot be a function since,&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-left: 30px"&gt;\(f({@aa*t_ans1*t_ans1s@}) = f({@aa@} \cdot {@t_ans1@} \cdot {@t_ans1s@}) = {@bb@} \cdot {@t_ans1@} + {@cc@} \cdot {@t_ans1s@} = {@bb*t_ans1 + cc*t_ans1s@}\)
&lt;br&gt;\(f({@aa*t_ans2*t_ans2s@}) = f({@aa@} \cdot {@t_ans2@} \cdot {@t_ans2s@}) = {@bb@} \cdot {@t_ans2@} + {@cc@} \cdot {@t_ans2s@} = {@bb*t_ans2 + cc*t_ans2s@}\)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;So, since \({@bb*t_ans1 + cc*t_ans1s@} \neq {@bb*t_ans2 + cc*t_ans2s@}\), \(f\) is &lt;b&gt;not&lt;/b&gt; a well-defined function.&lt;/p&gt;</text>
    </generalfeedback>
